<h2 id='key-features'>⚙️ Key Features for Jewelry and Watch Boutiques</h2>

<h3>Serialized Inventory Tracking</h3>

This is ProfitBooks' strongest card for the luxury sector. Every inventory item can carry a unique serial number, which means your Rolex Submariner with reference 126610LN gets its own record from the moment it enters your system as a purchase order through to the moment it leaves on a sales invoice. That audit trail is non-negotiable for high-value watch retail, and ProfitBooks handles it without requiring expensive add-ons or workarounds.

The system also supports batch tracking for jewelry lots — useful when you're receiving parcels of diamond melee or semi-finished pieces from a caster. You can assign a lot number, document cost basis at the lot level, and allocate costs to finished goods as they're assembled or mounted.

<h3>Multi-Currency Purchase Orders and Invoicing</h3>

Buying from Italian or Swiss brands in EUR, selling domestically in USD, and occasionally shipping to international customers? ProfitBooks handles multi-currency at both the PO and invoice level with automatic exchange rate lookups. Realized and unrealized FX gains/losses are tracked separately — a detail that most small-business accounting tools ignore until your accountant surfaces it at year-end.

Our team tested EUR-to-USD purchase orders against USD sales invoices and confirmed that the COGS calculation correctly used the exchange rate at the time of purchase, not the current rate. That's the right behavior, and not every platform gets it right.

<h3>COGS Reporting by Product and Category</h3>

For a jeweler, knowing that your branded watch segment runs at 28% gross margin while your estate jewelry segment runs at 61% is the kind of intelligence that shapes your entire buying strategy. ProfitBooks generates margin reports at the item, category, and time-period level without requiring you to export to Excel and build pivot tables.

The P&L statement is inventory-aware by default — it pulls COGS from actual landed costs recorded at the purchase order stage, not from standard costs you entered manually. That means your financial statements reflect reality, which is both better accounting and better decision-making.

<h3>Consignment Tracking</h3>

Consignment is endemic in fine jewelry, and it's one of the most accounting-messy arrangements a boutique can operate. ProfitBooks handles consignment as a distinct inventory status: pieces received on consignment appear in your stock count but carry a separate ownership flag, and they don't hit your balance sheet as assets until you record a sale. When you sell a consignment piece and remit to the vendor, ProfitBooks generates the remittance automatically from the sale record.

We compared this workflow against how most boutiques handle consignment in QuickBooks — usually a mess of manual journal entries and memorized transactions — and the ProfitBooks approach is materially cleaner.

<h3>Purchase Orders and Vendor Management</h3>

The PO workflow is complete: create, send, receive partial shipments, track backorders, and close. Vendor payment terms are stored at the vendor record level and auto-populate on new POs. For boutiques managing 15–40 active vendor relationships across branded goods, estate sources, and finished jewelry suppliers, this structure keeps payables organized without a dedicated AP person.

One limitation worth noting: ProfitBooks doesn't have a native vendor portal, so your brand reps can't log in to check PO status. That's a feature more common in enterprise ERP and less expected at this price point, but it's worth knowing.

<h3>Tax Configuration and Reporting</h3>

U.S. sales tax for jewelry is complicated by state-level exemptions on certain precious metal purchases and varying rules on jewelry repair labor. ProfitBooks allows product-level tax codes, which means you can configure your tax rules correctly rather than applying a blanket rate. The system generates tax liability reports by jurisdiction that your accountant or tax preparer can use directly.

For boutiques in states like Texas or Pennsylvania where precious metal sales may carry full exemption under certain conditions, getting tax configuration right at setup is critical. ProfitBooks' support team was responsive when we posed specific jewelry tax questions during our evaluation.

For more on tax compliance tools for boutiques, read our piece on <a href='https://luxeretailstack.com/guides/sales-tax-compliance-jewelry-retail'>sales tax compliance for jewelry retailers</a>.

